
                              FUNCTIONS
                              =========

There are 2 main types of functions - those which return a numeric value
and those which return a string value. Subdivided further into those
which take numeric or string arguments. However , for this document I
shall split them into return value types.

Numeric return
--------------
SQRT(<num>)
  Returns the square root of a number. The same result could be achieved by
  doing <num>^0.5.

ROUND(<num>)
  This rounds the number up or down to the nearest integer value.

SIN(<num>)
COS(<num>)
TAN(<num>)
  Return the sine,cosine and tangent of the argument given in degrees 
  (NOT radians!).

ASIN(<num>)
ACOS(<num>)
ATAN(<num>)
  Return the value in degrees of the arcsine, arcosine and arctan of the
  given values.

LOG(<num>)
LOG10(<num>)
  Return the natural log and the base10 log respectively.

HYPOT(<num1>,<num2>)
  Returns the hypotenuse of the 2 triangle sides given.

ISNUM(<string>)
  Returns 1 if the string is a valid number else 0.

EOF(#<stream>)
  Returns 1 if we're at the end of the given stream else 0.

HASDATA(#<stream>)
  Returns 1 if there is data waiting on the stream to be read. This is really
  meant to be used on stdin (#1) if using the CINPUT command. It doesn't 
  work properly with file and directory streams (the POSIX select()
  function returns data to read flag even if EOF is reached) so use EOF()
  instead for these.

WAITDATA(#<stream>[,<millisecond timeout>])
  This waits indefinately on a stream (unless the timeout is given) for data
  to arrive. It returns 1 if data is available or 0 if a timeout or error has
  occured.

SEARCH(<search string>,<searched for string>[,<start pos>])
  Searches for the searched for string starting at the optional start position
  (1 if not given) in the search string. If found it returns the position else
  it returns 0 (string positions start at 1).

LENGTH(<string>)
  Returns the length of the string.

WORDCNT(<string>)
  Returns a count of the number of words in the string. Words are defined as
  a group of characters bounded by whitespace (or the ends of the string).

VAL(<string>)
  Returns the numeric value of a string. If the string is not a valid number
  then an error is returned.

ASC(<string>[,<start pos>])
  Returns the asci value of the first character in the string starting at
  the start position or 1 if this is not supplied.

RAND()
  Returns a random number between 0 and 1.

TIME()
  Returns the number of seconds and microseconds since the unix epoch (1 jan
  1970). The microseconds are to the right of the decimal point.

FACT(<number>)
  Returns the factorial of a number. This works with floating point values too. 
  eg: >print fact(4.2)
      35.481600

MATCH(<string>,<pattern>)
  This returns 1 if the string matches the pattern , else 0. The pattern is
  a while card pattern (NOT a regular expression) and it uses the '*' and '?'
  operators. I did it this way because I can't stand regular expressions and
  for the simple matching that will probably be done in BASIC wildcard 
  patterns are fine.
  eg: print match("hello","h?llo")
      print match("this is a test","t*is?a t*")

String return
-------------

CHR$(<num>)
  Return the ascii character with the given numeric value. If num is < 0 or
  > 255 then an out of range error is returned.

MID$(<string>,<start pos>,<length>)
  This returns a portion of the given string starting at the start position
  and with the given length. 

LEFT$(<string>,<length>)
  This is the same as MID$() except that the start position is hard coded
  to be 1.

RIGHT$(<string>,<length>)
  This returns given length of the string starting from the right of the
  string.

FORMAT$(<format string>,<num>)
  This converts a number into a string using the format given. Try the
  examples below.
  eg: print format$("###,###,###.###",123456.789)
      print format$("XXX,XXX,XXX.XXX",123456.789)

TOSTR$(<num>)
  This converts a number into a string without you needing to supply a 
  formatting string.

PAD$(<string>,<num>)
  This returns the given string repeated the number of times given.

WORD$(<string>,<pos>)
  This returns the word at the given position in the string. As with WORDCNT()
  a word is defined as a group of characters bounded by whitespace or the ends
  of the string.

ERROR$(<error num>)
  Returns the string error message for the given error number.

DATE$(<time>)
  Returns the date string for the given time. Eg: print date$(time())

STAT$(<path>)
LSTAT$(<path>)
  These return information on objects in the filesystem. They only differ in
  how they treat soft links - STAT returns information on the file pointed to
  by the link, LSTAT returns information on the link itself.
  The return format of these functions is as follows:

  <type> <permissions> <owner> <group> <size> <last access> <last mod> \
         <last status change> <number of hard links>

  eg: >print stat$(current_dir$)
      DIR 0755 neil users 704 1144751572 1144751668 1144751668 5
      >print stat$("mylink")
      FILE 1432 neil users 20 1144751571 1144665225 1144748710 1
      >print lstat$("mylink")
      LINK 0777 neil users 6 1144751891 1144751885 1144751885 1
      >print lstat$("/dev/tty0")
      CHAR 0660 root root 0 1142011644 1142011644 1142670699 1

  The file types returned are the following:
      FILE   - normal file
      DIR    - directory
      LINK   - soft link
      FIFO   - Fifo
      BLOCK  - Block device
      CHAR   - Character device
      SOCKET - Unix socket

MAX(<num>,<num>[,<num>...])
MIN(<num>,<num>[,<num>...])
  These return the MAX or MIN of a list of numeric values. There must be at
  least 2 values passed in.
  eg: print max(1,3,2)

MAX$(<string>,<string>[,<string>...])
MIN$(<string>,<string>[,<string>...])
  These do the same as the above except for strings.

  eg: >print max$("d" & "ef",left$("hello",2),right$("wibble",2))
      le

MEAN(<num>,<num>[,<num>...])
  This returns the arithmetic mean (average) of the list of numbers.

GMEAN(<num>,<num>[,<num>...])
  This returns the geometric mean of the numbers which is Nth root of the 
  product of the values where N is the number of values:
     Eg: for values a,b & c the geometric mean = (a * b * c) ^ (1/3).
  The geometric mean is always less than the arithmetic mean.
  Note: If the product of the values is negative a result cannot be given as
  you cannot take a root of a negative number. Check online sources for more 
  info. 

MEDIAN(<num>,<num>[,<num>...])
  This returns the median of the list of numbers.

ARMAX(<numeric array>)
ARMIN(<numeric array>)
ARMEAN(<numeric array>)
ARGMEAN(<numeric array>)
ARMEDIAN(<numeric array>)
  These do the same as the MAX, MIN, MEAN, GMEAN and MEDIAN functions except 
  they take a numeric array as input instead of a list.

ARMAX$(<string array>)
ARMIN$(<string array>)
  These return the max and min of value of the given array.

UPPER$(<string>)
LOWER$(<string>)
  These convert the given string to upper and lower case respectively.

REVERSE$(<string>)
  This will reverse the given string. Personally I can't see much use for it
  but lots of other BASICs have it so I put it in anyway.
